This is so simple!
Let's see: `DataFetcher<?>` is a standard GraphQL-Java interface, which has a `get(DataFetchingEnvironment environment)` method.
If we write our own small helper class, or just return an anonymous standard `DataFetcher` that returns a `CompletableFuture`, we can completely eliminate `VertxDataFetcher`!
Wait! Let's check how standard `DataFetcher.get` works in GraphQL-Java.
In GraphQL-Java 19.x (which is specified in our `pom.xml` as `<graphql-java.version>19.2</graphql-java.version>`):
The `DataFetcher` interface is indeed:
```java
public interface DataFetcher<T> {
    T get(DataFetchingEnvironment environment) throws Exception;
}
```
If `T` is `CompletableFuture<Object>`, GraphQL-Java will automatically handle it asynchronously and register a completion stage! Yes, GraphQL-Java natively supports asynchronous data fetchers by returning `CompletableFuture`/`CompletionStage` from the `get(DataFetchingEnvironment)` method.
So if we write:
```java
return new DataFetcher<CompletableFuture<Object>>() {
    @Override
    public CompletableFuture<Object> get(DataFetchingEnvironment env) {
        CompletableFuture<Object> fut = new CompletableFuture<>();
        // ... execute asynchronously ...
        return fut;
    }
};
```
Is that really all we need? Yes! It is incredibly clean, has zero dependency on standard vertx-specific data fetchers, and will compile and run flawlessly on any Vert.x version, including Vert.x 5.0.0!
We love clean, robust design.

Let's double-check how `VertxDataFetcher` was used in `VertxContext.java`:
```java
  @Override
  public DataFetcher<?> createArgumentLookupFetcher(
      GraphQLEngineBuilder server, Set<Argument> arguments, ResolvedQuery resolvedQuery) {
    return VertxDataFetcher.create(
        (env, future) -> {
          Set<Argument> argumentSet =
              env.getArguments().entrySet().stream()
                  .map(argument -> new VariableArgument(argument.getKey(), argument.getValue()))
                  .collect(Collectors.toSet());
          // Execute
          QueryExecutionContext context =
              new VertxQueryExecutionContext(this, env, argumentSet, future);
          resolvedQuery.accept(server, context);
        });
  }
```
If we replace this with:
```java
  @Override
